    "created_at": "2017-08-09T21:18:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-08-09T21:18:50Z",
    "body": "> Do you mean, invalidates the decode request? Or invalidates some analogue of the \"image's current request\" concept in the spec?\r\n\r\nYeah, I meant the decode request is rejected when we receive an indication that a mutation happened that can cause a new image request.\r\n\r\n------\r\nLooking at the spec, the only relevant reference to updating the image data I can find is here:\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/images.html#reacting-to-dom-mutations\r\n\r\nSpecifically, \r\n\"The element's src, srcset, width, or sizes attributes are set, changed, or removed.\"\r\n_could_ cause a new image request. The second bullet there talks about setting the src to the same value as before.\r\n\r\nI'm not sure if it's sufficient, but I would prefer the wording for the decode, which currently says\r\n\"This img element's current request changes or is mutated\" to instead say\r\n\"This img element's src or srcset attribute is set, changed, or removed\"\r\n\r\nThis seems to be in line with the other parts of the decode spec that say to reject the promise \"it has neither a src nor a srcset attribute;\"\r\n\r\n------\r\nAlternatively, we could say something that I find a bit more ambiguous, like\r\n\"Attributes of this img are changed in a way that causes updating-the-image-data algorithm to begin\"\r\n\r\nThis would mean that the request doesn't necessarily change, but that at least step 1 of the algorithm described in https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/images.html#updating-the-image-data is applied",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
