    "created_at": "2017-08-14T16:10:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-08-14T16:10:28Z",
    "body": "I believe @zcorpan is back and so might be able to help us more here...\r\n\r\n> I'm not sure if it's sufficient, but I would prefer the wording for the decode, which currently says \"This img element's current request changes or is mutated\" to instead say \"This img element's src or srcset attribute is set, changed, or removed\"\r\n\r\nWell, per the section you point to, it's definitely not sufficient. We need to capture all the other relevant mutations as well. So maybe \"This img element undergoes any relevant mutations\"?\r\n\r\nBut even that may not be sufficient. I will repeat my above concern:\r\n\r\n> In particular I am concerned about cases like resizing the window and thus causing a different `<source>` in a `<picture>` to apply.\r\n\r\nSo, I'm not sure what to do.\r\n\r\n> This seems to be in line with the other parts of the decode spec that say to reject the promise \"it has neither a src nor a srcset attribute;\"\r\n\r\nYes, it seems like that part might be wrong also, given that an image can have a current source with no `src`/`srcset` attribute, when `picture` is involved...",
