    "created_at": "2017-08-21T16:39:07Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-08-21T16:39:07Z",
    "body": "Yeah, I agree. Maybe the best thing to do is to put this all in a microtask. This means that anything that mutates the request will be ordered consistently with the decode requests. \r\n\r\nThis basically means that we always return a promise when requested, whatever the state of the image is.\r\n\r\n(Just thinking out loud below)\r\n\r\nThe image could be in one of three states:\r\n1. No request (src/etc not set)\r\n2. Current request is being processed (onload hasn't fired)\r\n3. Current request is complete (onload fired, it's a finished image)\r\n\r\nAdditionally, each of those three states are further subdivided into whether they have a request-mutating task pending or not.\r\n\r\nWhen we run this image-decode microtask, then we do a different thing depending on the current state, but it doesn't really depend on whether there is a pending request-mutating task.\r\n- In state 1, we reject the promise.\r\n- In state 2, we enter the waiting phase (waiting for the image to complete)\r\n- In state 3, we enter the decode phase (send out the actual decode to happen)\r\n\r\nWhen a pending request-mutating task runs _and_ it modifies the current image, we reject the decodes in either the waiting or the decode phase. We know that we can't yet have a decode pending for the new image, because that would have been posted as a task that hasn't run yet. \r\n\r\nOverall seems pretty solid. Are there any cases that I'm missing?\r\n",
